In a C program, you can't directly terminate the program like you can in some other programming languages using a keyword or built-in function. Instead, you can use one of the following methods to terminate the execution of your C program:

1. Normal termination: You can use the `exit()` function to terminate the program in a controlled manner. This function takes an integer argument, which can be used to indicate the exit status of the program. For example:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

int main() {
    int *ptr;
    int size;

    printf("Enter the size of the array: ");
    scanf("%d", &size);

    ptr = (int *) malloc(size * sizeof(int));

    if (ptr == NULL) {
        printf("Memory allocation failed.\n");
        exit(1);
    }

    // Your code here

    free(ptr);
    exit(0);
}
```

2. Abnormal termination: If your program encounters an error or a fatal exception, it may terminate abnormally. In such cases, the operating system may kill the process, or the program may crash. To handle such situations, you can use signal handlers to perform cleanup tasks before the program terminates. For example:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <signal.h>

void sigint_handler(int sig) {
    printf("Received SIGINT signal.\n");
    exit(1);
}

int main() {
    signal(SIGINT, sigint_handler);

    printf("Press Ctrl+C to terminate the program.\n");

    while (1) {
        // Your code here
    }

    return 0;
}
```

In this example, the program registers a signal handler for the SIGINT signal (which is generated when the user presses Ctrl+C). The signal handler prints a message and terminates the program using the `exit()` function. The program then enters an infinite loop and waits for the user to press Ctrl+C to terminate it.
